            <ArticleTitle>Lowering HbA1c in type 2 diabetics results in reduced risk of coronary heart disease and all-cause mortality.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>45-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.pcd.2007.12.004</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="AIMS" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To assess to which extent lowering the level of HbA1c influences the 10-year risk of coronary heart disease (CHD) and all-cause mortality in patients with type 2 diabetes mellitus.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A literature search in Pubmed and Embase was performed, and the absolute risks in the results of the relevant and valid studies compared.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Each study shows that with a decreasing percentage of HbA1c, both the risk of developing CHD and the risk of mortality decrease.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Lowering HbA1c in type 2 diabetics decreases the absolute risk of developing CHD by 5-17%, as well as decreasing all-cause mortality by 6-15%.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
